  "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the resting heart rate of a randomly selected adult. Then we have $X\u223cN(\\mu ,{8}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<70).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu )=0.70.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.70 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.70 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.35 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.5+0.35 \\\\ P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.85 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8}) & =0.85 \\\\ \u03a6(\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8}) & =0.85 \\\\ \\frac{0.1\\mu }{8} & ={\u03a6}^{-1}(0.85).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{\u03a6}^{-1}(0.85)=1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8} & =1.0364 \\\\ 0.1\\mu  & =8.2912 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{8.2912}{0.1} \\\\  & \u224882.912.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<70)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<70) & =P(Z<\\frac{70-82.912}{8}) \\\\  & \u2248P(Z<-1.61) \\\\  & =\u03a6(-1.61) \\\\  & =0.0537 \\\\  & \u22485.37%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $5.37%$ of the adults have a resting heart rate lower than $70\\,\\text{bpm}.$",
